{"text":"Recent work supports testing multi-negative sampled softmax while retaining NextItNet’s causal dilated-convolution backbone. [Wu et al., 2024](https://wujcan.github.io/papers/tois-ssm.pdf) show that temperature controls emphasis on hard negatives; excessively low temperature lets a few difficult examples dominate gradients. [Scalable Cross-Entropy, RecSys 2024](https://dspace.mit.edu/entities/publication/5392aca9-0bae-4176-9b3a-a8165957ba26) approximates catalog-wide cross-entropy by selecting informative outputs. [Correcting the LogQ Correction, 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.09331) distinguishes deterministically included positives from sampled negatives when correcting sampling bias. This correction addresses the sampling distribution, not false-negative identification.

Hardness needs controls: [AHNS, AAAI 2024](https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.05191) motivates adapting negative difficulty rather than always selecting the hardest items, although its experiments concern collaborative filtering. [Sequential sampling experiments, 2024](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.17276) find substantial head/mid/tail trade-offs: uniform negatives favor head items, while popularity-based sampling can improve balance at an overall accuracy cost. These results do not establish gains for Electronics or this evaluator.

Experiment implication: compare multi-negative sampled softmax against the current objective within one independently trained NextItNet per category. Exclude padding, the target, and every known training-history positive from negative pools, including in-batch candidates; deduplicate sampled IDs. This prevents known-positive collisions but cannot identify all latent positives. Test moderate temperatures and a random/semi-hard sampling mixture after warm-up; these are proposed ablations, not established optimal settings. Preserve left-padded final-position readout, seeds and training limits. Select by fixed four-category macro validation HR@10; inspect Electronics separation only as supporting diagnosis, since larger sampled training margins alone do not demonstrate generalization."}
